%DEV-BANNER%{\b\fs22 Faro accountability}\par
Faro looks at what is on this computer's screen, detects explicit content on the computer itself, and sends the allies you chose an encrypted report with heavily blurred thumbnails. Allies are also told if monitoring stops, is tampered with, or has its settings changed.\par
{\b Every account on this computer will be monitored,} including accounts created later, other users signed in at the same time and remote desktop sessions. Switching accounts is not a way around Faro, and there is no per-account exception.\par
Everyone who signs in to this computer is told that it is monitored. If other people use it, tell them before you install.\par
{\b Before you continue:}\par
\bullet  Use a standard (not administrator) account day to day. If you let an ally keep the administrator password, you will not be able to uninstall Faro yourself, because uninstalling needs that password. You can still remove this computer in the Faro web app (Computers, Remove) or delete your account (Settings, Delete your account), and your allies are told either way. Agree with the ally in advance what happens if you want out.\par
\bullet  Remove accounts nobody uses, so nobody can use the computer without being monitored.\par
There is no quiet way to leave Faro: every way out tells your allies first. If using Faro was not your own choice, read "If this wasn't your choice" in the member guide before you continue.\par
When you enroll this computer, Faro lists its accounts so you can check who will be monitored. Uninstalling Faro (Settings, Apps) tells your allies first.\par
